WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:09.360
Well this is, I think, possibly the best board game&nbsp;
ever which is quite a bold claim. Many people talk&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:14.560
about this as just the perfect game; it involves&nbsp;
strategy and a little bit of luck as well because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.560 --> 00:00:22.080
of the role of the dice. It's a actually a game&nbsp;
which- people play together, there's talking all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:29.200
the time, there's trading going on. Sometimes&nbsp;
games suffer because the person playing that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:33.120
particular turn is the only person really being&nbsp;
involved and the others are sitting waiting for-&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.120 --> 00:00:39.280
This one involves everyone. This is called the&nbsp;
Settlers of Catan. It was created by a German&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.280 --> 00:00:47.160
dentist who also loved board games and it won&nbsp;
the Spiel des Jahres which is kind of Nobel Prize of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.160 --> 00:00:52.440
um gaming in Germany. It's the sort of game which&nbsp;
gets a mathematician immediately excited because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.440 --> 00:00:58.880
just look at the layout of this board. It's um&nbsp;
hexagons not squares, and hexagons are just such&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.880 --> 00:01:04.640
a beautiful shape and the tessellation of the&nbsp;
plane using hexagon so as soon as I saw this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.640 --> 00:01:09.680
game I I was getting very excited. But you'll&nbsp;
see there are also numbers on here, so let me tell&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.680 --> 00:01:14.000
you a little bit about how you play the game. So&nbsp;
you'll see there are different sorts of squares;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:21.440
there's woodland here and woodland will generate a&nbsp;
different resource of logs. The mine hexagons give&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.440 --> 00:01:30.560
you ore; the pasture hexagons allow you to graze&nbsp;
sheep; the fields then we can grow some wheat; and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.560 --> 00:01:36.360
then in the hills you can dig out stuff to make&nbsp;
bricks. And each of these will allow you to build&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.360 --> 00:01:44.240
different things. Now how do you get these? Well if&nbsp;
you own a property and it's on say the corner of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.240 --> 00:01:49.880
these hexagons; we're going to see which of these&nbsp;
is going to generate some resources in this turn,

00:01:49.880 --> 00:01:58.720
and I'm going to throw some dice and I've got 10.
So those areas of land which have the number 10&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.720 --> 00:02:05.560
on them will be generating - uh in this case 10&nbsp;
is generating a sheep, so this will generate a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.560 --> 00:02:10.960
sheep for anybody who's got a property on the the&nbsp;
corner of this land. But unfortunately I I've not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.960 --> 00:02:18.360
generated anything because I wanted a 5, 4
and 11. So if I'd been here I would have got some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.360 --> 00:02:25.200
sheep resources. Interestingly, what's the chance&nbsp;
of getting - for example if I'm here - of getting a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:33.160
12? Unfortunately there's only one way to get a&nbsp;
12 with two dice which is a 6 and a 6. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.160 --> 00:02:37.800
this is what this little dot at the bottom here&nbsp;
is telling me, that this is actually quite a hard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.800 --> 00:02:43.680
thing to generate resources from. So is it a good&nbsp;
idea to put your property here? Because there's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:51.680
only one chance. Well look at 6 for example, this&nbsp;
has got five dots underneath it - why? Because well&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.680 --> 00:02:56.600
to get a 6 how many different ways can I get&nbsp;
a 6? There are five because I can get a 5

00:02:56.600 --> 00:03:05.680
and a 1, or a 4 and a 2, or 3 and&nbsp;
a 3, 2 and a 4, or a 1 and a 5. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.680 --> 00:03:10.880
there are five different ways to get a 6; four&nbsp;
different ways to get a a 5. So these little&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.880 --> 00:03:17.720
dots actually help you to do the probability in&nbsp;
this game, because um let's see. You know the if I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.720 --> 00:03:26.520
put a property here I've got 1 + 5, 6 plus another&nbsp;
4, that's 10 kind of different ways the role of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.520 --> 00:03:32.200
the dice can generate things for me here. Um let's&nbsp;
choose somewhere else and just calculate uh; let's let&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.200 --> 00:03:40.120
let's go here. That w as 10, so here we got 3 + 2&nbsp;
+ 4 - that's only 9. So this is a less strategic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.120 --> 00:03:45.440
place to put your property because only 9
out of the 36 different things are going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.440 --> 00:03:50.280
generate resources. So this is what you need to&nbsp;
do when you're playing Catan. So a little bit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.280 --> 00:03:55.640
of mathematical strategy here will help you to&nbsp;
choose which corners to go on. Because you want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.640 --> 00:04:01.600
to add up those dots cos that's telling you um&nbsp;
the probability, you're going to add up those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.600 --> 00:04:05.360
probabilities that you're going to generate some&nbsp;
resources if you put your property there. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.360 --> 00:04:09.480
is why a little bit of mathematics can help you&nbsp;to play this game.
- (Brady: There must be more to it than)&nbsp;

00:04:09.480 --> 00:04:13.920
(just who can crunch the numbers the best though&nbsp;
to win this game or?)
- Well there's actually a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.920 --> 00:04:17.880
of psychology involved, because once you've&nbsp;
got resources you might have found you've just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.880 --> 00:04:21.800
generated a lot of sheep, and what are you going&nbsp;
to do with all these sheep? And you need to start&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.800 --> 00:04:26.640
trading. So that's actually why this game is so&nbsp;
beautiful because it involves a lot of conversations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.640 --> 00:04:31.200
and "I'll give- I've got loads of sheep but I&nbsp;
want some some some logs, will you trade me?"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.200 --> 00:04:36.520
And there's a little- and that's not determined by&nbsp;
the game, that's determined by how much you need&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.520 --> 00:04:42.560
something and how much you can see your opponent&nbsp;
needs things. 
- Oh do you have any clay? Will you trade&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.560 --> 00:04:47.440
a rock for a clay? 
- But let me talk about another&nbsp;
mathematical aspect of the game which I think is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.440 --> 00:04:53.120
rather beautiful. It's not about strategy whilst&nbsp;
playing the game, but it's about how you set the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.120 --> 00:04:59.920
game up. Because if you notice this is a lot of&nbsp;
hexagons placed down on the board. And I place&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.920 --> 00:05:05.640
it down in a particular way, we've got the mines uh&nbsp;
placed here, there are three mines here. But the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.640 --> 00:05:13.400
beauty of this game is each time you set it up you&nbsp;
can lay the hexagons down in a new way each time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.400 --> 00:05:18.920
(Cos these are like- these are modular)
- They are! So you know we could have set this up such that uh&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.920 --> 00:05:23.640
this was uh swapped over. And so actually perhaps&nbsp;
there's a lot of woodland, you know we could have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.640 --> 00:05:29.040
a a whole great big forest here. So the numbers&nbsp;
actually must stay the same, although they can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.040 --> 00:05:34.520
actually rotate. So I was intrigued about, you&nbsp;
know, well how many different games are in this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.520 --> 00:05:41.520
box? You know Monopoly you only get one board so&nbsp;
there's only one game but there are so many games&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.520 --> 00:05:47.440
inside this box because of the permutations you&nbsp;
can do. So um I thought it might be fun with your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.440 --> 00:05:52.440
Numberphile audience just to calculate how many&nbsp;
different boards there- there can be.
- (The hexagons)

00:05:52.440 --> 00:05:57.200
(can go- can they go anywhere? Is there any rules&nbsp;
or restrictions?)
- No there are none at all so-&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.200 --> 00:06:01.600
(It sounds like it would be quite easy then)
- It is&nbsp;quite easy but there's a little bit of subtlety&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.600 --> 00:06:07.920
here because obviously- so you'd say okay, how many&nbsp;
squares are there?
- (Well hexagons, how many-)

00:06:07.920 --> 00:06:15.200
Oh my gosh yes, there's like-
- (How many hex-)
- How&nbsp;many hexagons are there?
- (Okay: 3, 7, what's)

00:06:15.200 --> 00:06:25.480
(that 12, 16 - is that 19?)
- 19 and these are called&nbsp;
hex numbers. So hex numbers go 1, 7, 19 and it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.480 --> 00:06:31.440
the number of hexagons you need. You know we&nbsp;
made- there are expansion of Catan, this is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.440 --> 00:06:38.160
basic version. Little challenge for Numberphile&nbsp;
 viewers: uh what's the formula for you know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.160 --> 00:06:46.960
like the a Catan board with n layers? There's a&nbsp;
way you can generate these hex numbers. The hex&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.960 --> 00:06:53.320
number for this board is 19. There are actually&nbsp;
19 factorial different ways can lay the hexagons&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.320 --> 00:06:58.600
down. So, you know, I can- let's- we can start in the&nbsp;
middle; there are 19 hexagons, I can put the first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.600 --> 00:07:03.840
one down there then we we go around in a circle&nbsp;
and then do this circle. So at first it looks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.840 --> 00:07:09.480
like there are 19 factorial different games you&nbsp;
can play in the Settlers of Catan. But you have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.480 --> 00:07:16.400
to remember some of these hexagons are actually&nbsp;
the same because I've got three different hexagons&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.400 --> 00:07:22.160
which are mines. So actually I could have put these&nbsp;
down like this and the board hasn't really changed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.160 --> 00:07:27.640
It means that these three mines can be permuted&nbsp;
around and I don't really get a new game. So I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.640 --> 00:07:33.880
need to divide that 19 factorial by 3 factorial&nbsp;
for the mines. Now the other resources, there are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.880 --> 00:07:40.480
I think 4 um - oh no let's see, look, there are&nbsp;
only 3 of the hills so that's another divide&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.480 --> 00:07:44.960
by 3 factorial. The other ones are all 4
so there are 3 of those, so I need divide by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.960 --> 00:07:51.760
3 factorial, 3 factorial, 4 factorial, 4
factorial, 4 factorial. And that will give me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.760 --> 00:07:59.960
uh the total number of- so let's just add that&nbsp;
up, make sure that uh 4 + 4 + 4 -- 12, plus 3,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.960 --> 00:08:06.360
15 + 3, 18 and you get- but there are 19. And that's&nbsp;
because there's actually a little desert. If you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.360 --> 00:08:11.760
generate the number 7, you'll see that 7
isn't anywhere here, it's the most common throw of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.760 --> 00:08:17.880
the dice. And so that has a different function in&nbsp;
the game. So that's given us our 19 hexagons, 19&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.880 --> 00:08:24.960
factorial divided by 3 factorial squared, divided by&nbsp;
4 factorial cubed. It's the total number of ways&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.960 --> 00:08:30.680
I can lay these hexagons down.
- (What number does&nbsp;that give us?)
- It gives us uh a very large number&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.680 --> 00:08:36.600
and I think I remember when I calculated it, if&nbsp;
we played a different game each night ever since&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.600 --> 00:08:41.720
the dinosaurs were around we'd still be playing&nbsp;
on different boards. So so that's quite a lot of-&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.720 --> 00:08:50.120
in fact let's let's look it up cos I did actually&nbsp;
calculate it in my new book about games! Um here we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.120 --> 00:09:00.040
go, my little hexagons. There are actually 244&nbsp;
billion, 432 million, 188 thousand different boards in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.040 --> 00:09:05.360
that game. Actually there is a little few more&nbsp;
subtleties to this um which you have to take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.360 --> 00:09:11.880
into account. So um the way these numbers can be&nbsp;
laid on the board, actually it's a fixed way but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.880 --> 00:09:18.280
they can be rotated in six different ways. So&nbsp;
actually you- you have to multiply up by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.280 --> 00:09:23.760
another 6. Um and then there's another subtlety&nbsp;
which I haven't talked about which are little&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.760 --> 00:09:29.240
ports that you can land in and they do different&nbsp;
things in the game which also determine another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.240 --> 00:09:34.720
few factors more of the way that you can play&nbsp;
this game. So there are a lot of games contained&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.720 --> 00:09:41.960
in this one single box.
(Is Catan a game where&nbsp;people debate strategy or?)
- Yeah there's a lot of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.960 --> 00:09:49.320
debate of strategy about where to place um your&nbsp;
properties as you're settling Catan. And so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.320 --> 00:09:54.200
that's important to look at these numbers because&nbsp;
you want to go places where you're more likely to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.200 --> 00:10:00.240
be generating resources because of where you've&nbsp;
placed your uh properties. Um there's also uh&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.240 --> 00:10:05.640
another part of the game which is laying roads;
so you're interested in in making the longest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.640 --> 00:10:12.520
road and that wins you things in the game. And so&nbsp;
again there's an interesting strategy about um how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.520 --> 00:10:18.480
am I actually going to lay roads through this&nbsp;
tessellation of hexagons? Um what's the longest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.480 --> 00:10:23.960
road that's possible um in the settlers of Catan?
That's another nice little mathematical challenge.&nbsp;&nbsp;

