I mean a lot of mathematicians love playing games.&nbsp;
But I think you'll find that many mathematicians&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.480 --> 00:11:02.040
do not like a game which involves any element of&nbsp;
chance. They like it to be pure strategy, so games&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.040 --> 00:11:09.360
like chess or the the game of Go, Chinese game&nbsp;
of Go. Intriguingly though, chess originally came&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.360 --> 00:11:16.920
from India and it actually involved dice. So the&nbsp;
early versions of chess, you would throw a dice in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.920 --> 00:11:23.160
order to determine which piece you were allowed&nbsp;
to move. And then the use of dice and gambling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.160 --> 00:11:28.440
was banned in India and people had to take these&nbsp;
dice away and then they thought, actually we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.440 --> 00:11:34.240
can just play this as a pure strategy game. So I&nbsp;
think people uh might be surprised to learn - one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.240 --> 00:11:39.680
of the things I learned when writing this book&nbsp;
about games - is that um actually dice were part&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:39.680 --> 00:11:45.400
of chess originally. 
- (Have you ever played a game&nbsp;
of chess using that rule? That'd be amazing)

